{ In the Najdorf, black plays 5. ...a6. Play it! }
1... a6 { This move prevents any white pieces from coming to the well-protected b5 square. None of that is possible now :) } { [%csl Ga6,Gb5,Bf1,Bd4,Bc3][%cal Ga7a6,Ga6b5] } 2. Be3 { This is what white plays most of the time. Now, you will push for e5, grabbing space, and kicking out the knight. } { [%cal Be7e5] } 2... e5 { [%csl Ge5,Rd4][%cal Ge7e5] } 3. Nb3 { Good! Now you will continue with Be6, Be7, and the knight on b8 goes to d7, so you can put your rook on c8.
One advantage you have is the open c file, you should put your pieces there, and also push for b5, gaining more space and attacking on the queenside. This gives black a solid fighting chance: Najdorf games are usually very sharp and tactical! Bobby Fischer used it. Plans are highlighted. } { [%cal Gc8e6,Gf8e7,Bb8d7,Ge8g8,Yb7b5] } *

